# STUDENT SLIDESHOW FORGE — Creación Digital I / II

The student-facing surface is a short image-led Reveal.js deck, visually
consistent with “How to Pass This Track.” It is not the lesson text, a research
ledger, or an instructor script. The fuller unit lesson and session prompt are
professor-side preparation material; internal provenance remains source-only.

## Media intake: collection → accepted pack → deck

Never read a raw Profield discovery index directly into a public page. The
correct route is:

```text
media-index.json (review inbox)
→ media-collection.py named collection (editorial snapshot)
→ instructor review / acceptance
→ MP5 YAML pack in docs/_data/media-packs/
→ student deck slots matched by media_slot_id
```

Use the unit manifests under
`/Users/ruvebal/src/profield/runs/media-prospector/<UNIT>/<RUN>/manifest.json`,
not the root index, as the asset source. The accepted I.1 snapshot is
`I.1/20260904T130000Z/manifest.json`: two reviewed Wikimedia Commons images,
both CC BY-SA 4.0. They seed the first I.1 analysis deck. All other unit decks
remain blocked until their own accepted manifest/collection arrives; never
invent a replacement image, URL, credit, licence, or alt text.

Each deck slide receives only public-safe media fields: title, public preview or
embed URL, alt text, credit line, licence display, and the single sentence that
states what students should notice. Profield run IDs, local paths, retrieval
traces, review notes, rights uncertainty, and scholarly provenance never render.

## Production rehydration

Background selection is collection-driven. For each unit, the rehydrator reads
the accepted assets belonging to that unit's reviewed media collection, sorts
them by upstream priority/rank, and assigns the selected asset to the slide's
`media_slot_id`. The slot names the teaching role; it does not itself fetch an
image. A slide with no media slot keeps the theme background.

### Explicit slide-image override

The deck author may force a particular accepted catalogue asset for a specific
slide. Use this when the teaching argument depends on one named image rather
than on whichever asset currently ranks first:

```json
{
  "media_selection": {
    "unit_id": "I.1",
    "collection": "designers",
    "strategy": "explicit-then-ranked"
  },
  "media_overrides": {
    "I.1.still.profield-1": "wikimedia:File:Fan SAAM-1935.8.20 1.jpg",
    "I.1.still.profield-2": "wikimedia:File:Miss Maquillage Digital Fashion Garment example.png"
  }
}
```

The key is the slide's `media_slot_id`; the value is the Profield `asset_id`.
The rehydrator must resolve the override inside the selected reviewed
collection, require `review_status: accepted`, a rank from 1–5, a usable
public image or video URL, rights evidence, and accessibility metadata. A
missing, rejected, unranked, or non-renderable override is never silently
published: report it and fall back to the normal accepted unit/rank selection
for that slot. Overrides take precedence only for their named slots; all
other slots remain collection- and rank-driven. Preserve the asset's canonical
source and licence metadata in the deck output.

Priority is marked upstream in the unit manifest (`review.priority`, `priority`,
or `rank`). Run `npm run media:rehydrate` before the production build. The
script scans accepted manifests under `runs/media-prospector/`, deduplicates by
asset ID, sorts by priority, preserves the deck's `media_slot_id` mapping, and
copies only public-safe fields into the deck JSON. The production deck has no
reload control and no dependency on Profield paths at runtime.

## Unit deck shape

One ordinary unit deck uses 5–7 slides. A conceptual masterclass such as I.0
may use 8–12 slides when the taxonomy requires it, but the one-idea rule still
holds: one conceptual move, one heading, one sentence, and one visual focus per
slide.

1. **Question:** unit title + one heading + one sentence.
2. **Look closely:** one accepted image/video and one observation question.
3. **Analyse:** compare two accepted assets or one asset and a teacher-made
   diagram; students write an individual reading before discussion.
4. **Concept:** one heading and one sentence; a teacher explains the criterion.
5. **Problem:** one constrained individual exercise and its submission trace.
6. **Project/workshop:** the collaborative or project move for that moment.
7. **Carry forward:** which portfolio evidence is saved and why.

Use visual-forger for course-generated diagrams. Use media-embed-forger only
after the MP5 pack passes validation. A student deck may name UEM and the course
only; it must not name Profield, Ahmes, Athanor, DevIAC, UDIT, Web Atelier, or
the sibling runtime.

## 120-minute class rhythm

The deck and professor guide follow this four-part rhythm:

| Minutes | Learning move | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 00–30 | Individual visual analysis, then comparison | annotated reading / short position |
| 30–60 | Lecture and demonstration | conceptual notes / criterion |
| 60–90 | Individual exercises and problem-solving | saved problem response for portfolio evidence |
| 90–120 | Project-based learning or workshop | group/project trace, critique, next task |

The final quarter changes with the unit: it may be a workshop, group project,
or project milestone. B3 remains individually attributable even when its
evidence later supports a collective project.

## Course delivery and assessment choreography

The following is a teaching design that must be mapped to the official UEM
assessment instruments; it does not change their 55 / 15 / 20 / 10 weighting
without formal course authorization.

| Course evidence | Student task | Evidence retained |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deliverable 1 — Analysis | Individual free-choice analysis, presented in person; support may be photo, drawing, illustration, or GIF | presentation support + individual analysis |
| Deliverable 2 — Transposition | Translate a self-chosen input from one medium/language into another; output is an image series | source declaration + image series + process trace |
| Deliverable 3 — Multimedia presentation | Multimedia work prepared for a live atrium show | multimedia export + technical/accessibility plan + group/individual roles |
| Final exam — public portfolio defence | Oral defence of the personal portfolio assembled from collected unit problem-solving evidence and course deliverables | portfolio index + selected exercises + oral defence support |

Deliverable 1’s internal rubric is: presentation 70%, deliverable 20%, and
participation in peers’ presentations/debate 10%. Schedule one or two students
per session for 10–15 minutes each. This is a rubric within the deliverable,
not a replacement for the official course-wide evaluation table.

## Required student-deck fields

```yaml
course_id: dci | dcii
unit_id: I.1 | II.4
slide_id: stable-unit-purpose-id
heading: "Short student-facing heading"
sentence: "One sentence only."
media_slot_id: I.1.still.editorial-vs-commercial
observation_prompt: "What decision changes the meaning here?"
portfolio_trace: "Save the annotated comparison in your portfolio index."
```

No slide is published until `media_slot_id` resolves to an accepted MP5 record
or the slide is a course-generated diagram with accessible text equivalent.

## Local Ollama forge phase

The ontology lecture and student slideshow use the locally installed model:

```bash
OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3.8:27b
```

Use the Ollama HTTP API with an explicit timeout. The model may draft lecture
structure, slide sentences, ontology-card prompts, and media-slot requests; it
may not invent citations, licences, image URLs, provenance, or rights status.
The deterministic forge remains authoritative for official contents, timing,
publication safety, visual-readability rules, and MP5 acceptance. If the model
is unavailable, fail closed rather than silently substituting another model.
